\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.1.1}The Lipid Membrane: A Selective Biological Landscape}{27}{subsection.1.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {1.1.1.1}Beyond Protein Targets: The Membrane as a Vulnerability}{28}{subsubsection.1.1.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Comparative analysis of lipid membrane profiles.} The left panel illustrates the lipid composition across different biological models. In a healthy mammalian membrane, lipids are distributed asymmetrically: the outer leaflet consists primarily of neutral phosphatidylcholines (blue), while the inner leaflet is enriched with phosphatidylethanolamines (gray) and anionic phosphatidylserines (green), resulting in a net negative charge on the internal surface. In cancer cells, this structural asymmetry is lost and lipids become more evenly distributed, leading to the acquisition of a negative charge on the external surface. Bacterial membranes (e.g., \textit  {E. coli}) exhibit a different composition, containing phosphatidylethanolamines and anionic phosphatidylglycerols (red) in both the interior and exterior leaflets, which imparts a significant negative charge to the cell surface. The right panel displays the chemical structures of the primary lipids studied: POPC, POPS, POPG, and POPE (from top to bottom). The schematic representation was generated using Matplotlib, while the molecular structures were rendered in ChemDraw.}}{29}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:Lipids}{{1.1}{29}{\textbf {Comparative analysis of lipid membrane profiles.} The left panel illustrates the lipid composition across different biological models. In a healthy mammalian membrane, lipids are distributed asymmetrically: the outer leaflet consists primarily of neutral phosphatidylcholines (blue), while the inner leaflet is enriched with phosphatidylethanolamines (gray) and anionic phosphatidylserines (green), resulting in a net negative charge on the internal surface. In cancer cells, this structural asymmetry is lost and lipids become more evenly distributed, leading to the acquisition of a negative charge on the external surface. Bacterial membranes (e.g., \textit {E. coli}) exhibit a different composition, containing phosphatidylethanolamines and anionic phosphatidylglycerols (red) in both the interior and exterior leaflets, which imparts a significant negative charge to the cell surface. The right panel displays the chemical structures of the primary lipids studied: POPC, POPS, POPG, and POPE (from top to bottom). The schematic representation was generated using Matplotlib, while the molecular structures were rendered in ChemDraw}{figure.caption.1}{}}
